nly a risk at this point  but it's also to be taken into account  along with india's high zero prevalence  rate which is uh  near about 80 percent uh going by the  last round  and that could be a blessing in disguise  and of course as far as i understand now  there's also a vaccine approved for kids  now how big a difference will this make  for all those festivals coming up  well it's only in the emergency  authorization stage  it's quite a distance from what was  announced today the second vaccine to be  approved uh to an actual rollout to a  vaccination program for kids that's  quite a bit of a journey but certainly  um it was a good news today and there's  a lot of optimism particularly as far as  children and adolescents go okay so  there's light at the end of the tunnel  there too but how worried are you that  this festival of durga puja could  eventually be a corona super spreader  event  well actually we don't know it's going  to be an interaction of two things one  a pretty high zero prevalence  which  is on account of a combination of two  things a reasonably good vaccination  coverage till this point particularly in  urban areas  plus uh immunity  post uh the delta tsunami that we had  which of course  came with its share of very  high levels of mortality in some parts  but they put two but the two put  together actually means that a  substantial proportion of the population  is protected but what's worrying is that  india currently has the largest  numbers of um  those individuals immunized with a  single vaccine that's partially  immunized population that in itself  is potentially worrisome plus the crowds  of course  worrisome because it could  promote mutants or worries and why  because  the protection is  not  that accorded by two doses of the  vaccine a very large proportion is still  partially immunized  not so much because a mutant would arise  in a very short time but yes if  transmission goes on the probability of  a mutant emerging does remain  